<article class="env-lemma" id="08RA">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08RA">Lemma <span data-tag="08RA">92.11.2</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a surjective ring map with kernel $I$. Then $H^0(L_{B/A}) = 0$ and $H^{-1}(L_{B/A}) = I/I^2$. This isomorphism comes from the map (<a href="/tag/08R9" data-tag="08R9">92.11.1.2</a>) for the object $(A \to B)$ of $\mathcal{C}_{B/A}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We will show below (using the surjectivity of $A \to B$) that there exists a short exact sequence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to \pi ^{-1}(I/I^2) \to \mathcal{J}/\mathcal{J}^2 \to \Omega \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of sheaves on $\mathcal{C}_{B/A}$. Taking $L\pi _!$ and the associated long exact sequence of homology, and using the vanishing of $H_1(\mathcal{C}_{B/A}, \mathcal{J}/\mathcal{J}^2)$ and $H_0(\mathcal{C}_{B/A}, \mathcal{J}/\mathcal{J}^2)$ shown in Remark <a href="/tag/08R7" data-tag="08R7">92.11.1</a> we obtain what we want using Lemma <a href="/tag/08QE" data-tag="08QE">92.4.4</a>. 
    <p>
      What is left is to verify the local statement mentioned above. For every object $U = (P \to B)$ of $\mathcal{C}_{B/A}$ we can choose an isomorphism $P = A[E]$ such that the map $P \to B$ maps each $e \in E$ to zero. Then $J = \mathcal{J}(U) \subset P = \mathcal{O}(U)$ is equal to $J = IP + (e; e \in E)$. The value on $U$ of the short sequence of sheaves above is the sequence 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to I/I^2 \to J/J^2 \to \Omega _{P/A} \otimes _ P B \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Verification omitted (hint: the only tricky point is that $IP \cap J^2 = IJ$; which follows for example from More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0666" data-tag="0666">15.31.9</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>